        "result": "Saved `pelican.html` in the current directory (13.3 KB, fully self-contained — no scripts, fonts, or external assets).\n\n**What's in it**\n\n- **The pelican:** body, tail, S-curved neck, head with eye, a long upper mandible with an animated gulping pouch, a red cycling cap, one wing resting on the handlebar and a far wing that flaps, plus a gentle body bob synced to the pedal stroke.\n- **The bicycle:** frame (seat tube, seat stay, chain stay, down tube, head tube, fork), saddle, handlebar, two spoked wheels spinning, a toothed chainring, rear cog, crank arms, and a pedal that stays level while orbiting the bottom bracket.\n- **Mechanically correct pedalling:** the legs are drawn as hip → knee → ankle → toe paths whose keyframes were solved by two-link inverse kinematics against the crank circle, so the feet stay glued to the pedals and the knees bend forward. Both legs are 180° out of phase.\n- **The world:** sky gradient, rotating sun rays, seamlessly looping clouds, layered hills, passing bushes, and a dashed centre line scrolling the correct way (ground moves left, chain top run moves forward).\n\nAll motion is SMIL (`animateTransform`, `animate`, `animateMotion`) on a 1.2 s crank cycle, so the wheels, chain, pedals, legs and bobbing all stay in phase. The leg paths carry static initial `d` values, so the pelican still renders as a proper cyclist in viewers that ignore SMIL.",
